WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.260
&gt;&gt; Please welcome,
Global Managing Director

00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:14.450
Microsoft Research
Outreach, Sandy Blyth.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.730
&gt;&gt; Hello, good morning.

00:00:20.731 --> 00:00:22.365
&gt;&gt; Good morning.

00:00:22.366 --> 00:00:25.800
&gt;&gt; Good morning. Coffee is
served in the back there,

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:27.530
let's go grab some of
that. Good morning.

00:00:27.531 --> 00:00:28.970
It's really a pleasure for me and

00:00:28.971 --> 00:00:30.975
a privilege to welcome you,

00:00:30.976 --> 00:00:33.080
and to be your host for

00:00:33.081 --> 00:00:35.210
this North American
Faculty Summit.

00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:36.710
It was great to see

00:00:36.711 --> 00:00:38.450
so many of you at
the reception last night.

00:00:38.451 --> 00:00:39.530
I hope you enjoyed that.

00:00:39.531 --> 00:00:40.985
I saw a lot of old friends,

00:00:40.986 --> 00:00:42.050
and a lot of new faces,

00:00:42.051 --> 00:00:44.330
so we're really excited
to be hosting you

00:00:44.331 --> 00:00:47.030
this week at Faculty Summit.

00:00:47.031 --> 00:00:52.370
It's a really busy time of
the year for you. I know that.

00:00:52.371 --> 00:00:54.515
There's a crazy number
of things you

00:00:54.516 --> 00:00:57.145
could be doing at this point
in the academic year,

00:00:57.146 --> 00:01:00.520
plus some personal commitments
and opportunities.

00:01:00.521 --> 00:01:02.420
So, the fact that
you chose to spend

00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:03.560
a couple of days with us here in

00:01:03.561 --> 00:01:04.760
Redmond is a real privilege,

00:01:04.761 --> 00:01:06.690
and we do thank you
for being here.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:08.660
Faculty Summit for us is

00:01:08.661 --> 00:01:10.820
literally a year in
the making each year.

00:01:10.821 --> 00:01:12.910
So, it's something that we
circle on the calendar.

00:01:12.911 --> 00:01:15.580
It's a real highlight
for the folks at MSR.

00:01:15.581 --> 00:01:18.110
We're really dedicated to
making this worth your while.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:20.095
I hope it is.

00:01:20.096 --> 00:01:21.720
This is, well, it's

00:01:21.721 --> 00:01:25.330
the North America version
of our Faculty Summit.

00:01:25.331 --> 00:01:27.210
We do Faculty Summits
all around the world.

00:01:27.211 --> 00:01:28.840
This is our 19th here in

00:01:28.841 --> 00:01:32.050
Redmond and I hope that this
is one of the best of them.

00:01:32.051 --> 00:01:34.505
In addition to about 200

00:01:34.506 --> 00:01:36.360
academic researchers and faculty,

00:01:36.361 --> 00:01:39.870
we'll have a number of
Microsoft professionals.

00:01:39.871 --> 00:01:41.065
We are also streaming

00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:43.010
the plenary sessions
live over the web.

00:01:43.011 --> 00:01:45.170
We had several 100 registrations

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.790
for the planneries and we'll be

00:01:46.791 --> 00:01:48.980
recording the breakout sessions

00:01:48.981 --> 00:01:51.400
for replay and
access to later on.

00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:53.240
So, if there's something
that you really wanted to

00:01:53.241 --> 00:01:55.370
see and just couldn't get
to it while you were here,

00:01:55.371 --> 00:01:57.395
it'll be available
to you later on.

00:01:57.396 --> 00:01:59.730
This year's Faculty Summit,

00:01:59.731 --> 00:02:02.240
also we have
a very high percentage

00:02:02.241 --> 00:02:04.110
of our most senior
technical leadership,

00:02:04.111 --> 00:02:05.395
our technical fellows,

00:02:05.396 --> 00:02:07.620
distinguished scientists,
and engineers.

00:02:07.621 --> 00:02:09.290
So, we're really
looking forward to what

00:02:09.291 --> 00:02:11.100
we hope will be a rich,

00:02:11.101 --> 00:02:13.725
engaging dialogue with you.

00:02:13.726 --> 00:02:21.035
Well, this year, our Faculty
Summit topic is systems,

00:02:21.036 --> 00:02:22.775
and it's truly never been

00:02:22.776 --> 00:02:24.680
a better time to be
in systems research.

00:02:24.681 --> 00:02:26.375
There's so much going on

00:02:26.376 --> 00:02:28.790
that systems research is
going to be powering.

00:02:28.791 --> 00:02:31.160
With all of the
developments in the cloud,

00:02:31.161 --> 00:02:33.150
the proliferation of
different devices

00:02:33.151 --> 00:02:34.960
and experiences out on the edge,

00:02:34.961 --> 00:02:37.535
all that's going on in new
hardware and software,

00:02:37.536 --> 00:02:39.290
architectures have
to be rethought,

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:43.160
fundamentally new ways of
developing applications,

00:02:43.161 --> 00:02:44.530
the end of Moore's law.

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.030
There's so much going on here.

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:47.330
We really are excited about

00:02:47.331 --> 00:02:49.735
the topics that we'll be
able to show you today.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:53.090
We got to the Systems
Focus not only

00:02:53.091 --> 00:02:54.215
because of the focus and

00:02:54.216 --> 00:02:56.210
importance of systems
research today,

00:02:56.211 --> 00:02:57.650
but the feedback from you and

00:02:57.651 --> 00:02:59.590
your prior attendance
at Faculty Summit said,

00:02:59.591 --> 00:03:02.150
"We want to keep
the topics narrow."

00:03:02.151 --> 00:03:05.000
So, we've gone
narrow and deep with

00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:06.110
this topic as we did

00:03:06.111 --> 00:03:08.140
last year with
the artificial intelligence.

00:03:08.141 --> 00:03:09.610
We think that that leads to a

00:03:09.611 --> 00:03:11.420
richer, more engaged
conversation.

00:03:11.421 --> 00:03:13.610
So, each of you is
closely related

00:03:13.611 --> 00:03:16.460
to the research interests
of your colleagues.

00:03:16.461 --> 00:03:18.290
We've also limited
the attendance,

00:03:18.291 --> 00:03:20.605
so there's only about 200
folks here today.

00:03:20.606 --> 00:03:22.340
Again, we're very privileged

00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:24.255
that you would be
willing to join us.

00:03:24.256 --> 00:03:26.420
We'll find throughout the agenda

00:03:26.421 --> 00:03:28.040
that we're trying to build in

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:30.800
not only time for
deep and engaging conversations

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:32.090
but also some time to network.

00:03:32.091 --> 00:03:33.950
That also was feedback

00:03:33.951 --> 00:03:35.990
that we received from
you in the past.

00:03:35.991 --> 00:03:38.210
I hope that you find
Faculty Summit to be one of

00:03:38.211 --> 00:03:40.860
those really rare fora
that you can come to,

00:03:40.861 --> 00:03:44.760
that balances an intimacy
with a scholarly approach,

00:03:44.761 --> 00:03:46.750
that is rewarding
and interesting,

00:03:46.751 --> 00:03:49.280
and that you get to network
and meet new colleagues as

00:03:49.281 --> 00:03:52.295
well as renew friendships
with some old colleagues.

00:03:52.296 --> 00:03:54.650
Here's just a brief view
of the agenda,

00:03:54.651 --> 00:03:56.300
you've got this in
the handout, so we've got it.

00:03:56.301 --> 00:03:58.265
It's also available
to you on the web.

00:03:58.266 --> 00:03:59.600
Hopefully, you will see

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:00.980
here the kind of
balance that we're

00:04:00.981 --> 00:04:03.920
trying to achieve
between deep and broad.

00:04:03.921 --> 00:04:06.380
In addition, some
new things that we

00:04:06.381 --> 00:04:09.020
have based on feedback from
prior Faculty Summits,

00:04:09.021 --> 00:04:10.370
we're having
our technical fellows

00:04:10.371 --> 00:04:11.990
and distinguished scientists and

00:04:11.991 --> 00:04:13.250
engineer host something that we

00:04:13.251 --> 00:04:15.100
call One Table, One Topic.

00:04:15.101 --> 00:04:17.120
So, it's kind of a Birds
of a Feather session

00:04:17.121 --> 00:04:19.220
that we'll offer over the lunch.

00:04:19.221 --> 00:04:21.860
If there's a particular
topic or somebody at

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.270
MSR that you're particularly

00:04:23.271 --> 00:04:25.070
keen to spend a little bit
of extra time with,

00:04:25.071 --> 00:04:26.600
and maybe talk
about some areas of

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:28.360
interest or common research,

00:04:28.361 --> 00:04:30.810
look for those tables and
we'll also do this tomorrow.

00:04:30.811 --> 00:04:33.160
If you want to sign up
and host a bit of a Birds

00:04:33.161 --> 00:04:36.475
of a Feather over
the lunchtime, please do.

00:04:36.476 --> 00:04:39.935
We also are trying to help

00:04:39.936 --> 00:04:41.500
folks get to know each other

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.375
because there are
a lot of new faces,

00:04:43.376 --> 00:04:44.800
both at MSR and in

00:04:44.801 --> 00:04:46.730
the academic community,
they're attending today.

00:04:46.731 --> 00:04:50.225
So, we have these researcher
spotlights that help you get

00:04:50.226 --> 00:04:51.860
familiar with some of

00:04:51.861 --> 00:04:53.420
the research areas
of your colleagues

00:04:53.421 --> 00:04:55.135
as well as get to know
them a little bit.

00:04:55.136 --> 00:04:56.690
So, I, for example,

00:04:56.691 --> 00:04:59.645
learned that Laura Waller
has over 100 cousins.

00:04:59.646 --> 00:05:02.360
I thought my family reunions
where a food fight,

00:05:02.361 --> 00:05:04.770
but thank you Laura for
sharing that part of it.

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.290
It's super interesting
to know that about you.

00:05:07.291 --> 00:05:10.520
Faculty Summit around
the world tends

00:05:10.521 --> 00:05:13.100
to be kind of
a once a year event,

00:05:13.101 --> 00:05:15.889
but we are looking for
opportunities to try and maintain

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:17.610
a dialogue and collaboration and

00:05:17.611 --> 00:05:19.665
engagement across
the communities.

00:05:19.666 --> 00:05:21.140
So, here's a few ways that we

00:05:21.141 --> 00:05:22.850
hope that you can
keep in touch with

00:05:22.851 --> 00:05:24.860
Microsoft Research
and communicate

00:05:24.861 --> 00:05:26.310
with researchers in MSR.

00:05:26.311 --> 00:05:31.125
We're quite keen to have you
to engage with us this way.

00:05:31.126 --> 00:05:33.860
Well, as I say, it was great
to see so many of you at

00:05:33.861 --> 00:05:36.110
the reception last night.
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:05:36.111 --> 00:05:39.190
We're at the Hyatt Regency
down in Lake Washington.

00:05:39.191 --> 00:05:41.300
I had two jobs for
Faculty Summit,

00:05:41.301 --> 00:05:43.400
one of which was to
arrange for good weather.

00:05:43.401 --> 00:05:44.920
I'm thinking so far,

00:05:44.921 --> 00:05:46.610
we're doing okay because that was

00:05:46.611 --> 00:05:48.350
a spectacular venue last night.

00:05:48.351 --> 00:05:50.990
We'll be back there
for dinner tonight.

00:05:50.991 --> 00:05:53.630
I'm excited to be
able to host you at

00:05:53.631 --> 00:05:54.995
the dinner there this evening

00:05:54.996 --> 00:05:56.300
and I really do hope
you enjoyed it.

00:05:56.301 --> 00:05:57.885
It's a bit of a reveal,

00:05:57.886 --> 00:06:00.325
as you come in, when
you come driving in,

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:03.810
it looks like it is
a construction zone,

00:06:03.811 --> 00:06:04.830
it's a brand new hotel,

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.230
it's beautiful. I
hope you enjoy it.

00:06:06.231 --> 00:06:07.770
Let us know through
your feedback, though,

00:06:07.771 --> 00:06:08.900
if that's a great venue for

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:11.265
you and if you enjoyed
the time there.

00:06:11.266 --> 00:06:15.590
The Summit is all about
fostering collaboration, though.

00:06:15.591 --> 00:06:18.360
It's about scholarly
dialogue and exchange,

00:06:18.361 --> 00:06:20.360
but we want to make it
a special time as well.

00:06:20.361 --> 00:06:21.710
So, we'll be announcing

00:06:21.711 --> 00:06:23.715
some things here
for the first time.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:26.480
We'll also be providing
some exclusives to you to say

00:06:26.481 --> 00:06:29.435
thank you for joining us today.

00:06:29.436 --> 00:06:31.700
I actually have
the opportunity to do one

00:06:31.701 --> 00:06:33.725
of those reveals
here this morning.

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:36.440
So, I'd like to talk
to you about some of

00:06:36.441 --> 00:06:37.760
the Fellowship and Grant

00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:40.255
programs very briefly
that we have at MSR.

00:06:40.256 --> 00:06:42.215
Hopefully, most of
you are familiar with

00:06:42.216 --> 00:06:44.240
our North America PhD Fellowship.

00:06:44.241 --> 00:06:45.910
This is very similar to

00:06:45.911 --> 00:06:47.240
Fellowship Programs that we have

00:06:47.241 --> 00:06:48.910
another geographies
around the world.

00:06:48.911 --> 00:06:50.690
It's intended for PhD students in

00:06:50.691 --> 00:06:52.745
their third and fourth year

00:06:52.746 --> 00:06:55.850
to full tuition plus
an annual stipend.

00:06:55.851 --> 00:06:58.160
We also have what we refer to as

00:06:58.161 --> 00:07:00.735
our Dissertation Grant Program
in North America,

00:07:00.736 --> 00:07:02.810
which is aimed really
at fourth year students

00:07:02.811 --> 00:07:04.640
trying to get especially a

00:07:04.641 --> 00:07:06.760
diverse and underrepresented
populations

00:07:06.761 --> 00:07:08.230
over the hump to finally get

00:07:08.231 --> 00:07:12.220
their thesis and
their degree granted.

00:07:12.221 --> 00:07:13.740
To that, though, we've added

00:07:13.741 --> 00:07:16.540
two new fellowships that
we're announcing today,

00:07:16.541 --> 00:07:19.170
and details are available out
in the lobby at the booth,

00:07:19.171 --> 00:07:22.205
and will be released on
the web this morning.

00:07:22.206 --> 00:07:25.695
One of which we are calling
the Ada Lovelace Fellowship.

00:07:25.696 --> 00:07:27.650
This is a North American
Fellowship, again,

00:07:27.651 --> 00:07:30.920
aimed at diverse and
underrepresented populations

00:07:30.921 --> 00:07:32.285
in computer science.

00:07:32.286 --> 00:07:34.820
There are some particular
structural challenges that

00:07:34.821 --> 00:07:37.180
historically have
touched that group.

00:07:37.181 --> 00:07:39.095
So, what we're trying to do is

00:07:39.096 --> 00:07:41.310
get a little bit earlier
in their career.

00:07:41.311 --> 00:07:43.040
This is intended for

00:07:43.041 --> 00:07:44.930
a diverse and
underrepresented groups in

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:47.600
their second year of
their PhD program

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:50.765
and that scholarship
runs a full three years.

00:07:50.766 --> 00:07:54.810
As well, we're actually
bringing back an old friend,

00:07:54.811 --> 00:07:57.110
it's the Faculty
Fellows' program.

00:07:57.111 --> 00:07:59.110
So, this is intended for Faculty,

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:00.550
Young and Career Faculty.

00:08:00.551 --> 00:08:03.020
So, those who are within
the first five years of

00:08:03.021 --> 00:08:05.860
entering the professoriate
to try, and again,

00:08:05.861 --> 00:08:07.820
provide some additional funding
to get you through

00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:09.440
those first few years
as you're building

00:08:09.441 --> 00:08:11.555
up your research base.

00:08:11.556 --> 00:08:13.940
We're really excited
about these especially in

00:08:13.941 --> 00:08:17.085
an environment where
the funding can be difficult,

00:08:17.086 --> 00:08:19.640
to bring both our existing and

00:08:19.641 --> 00:08:22.655
some new student and
faculty programs, too.

00:08:22.656 --> 00:08:24.630
We're also bringing
some experiences.

00:08:24.631 --> 00:08:27.250
So, this year we'll be
having our first PhD forum,

00:08:27.251 --> 00:08:29.370
which will include PhD students

00:08:29.371 --> 00:08:31.665
from the Fellowship,
the Dissertation Grant.

00:08:31.666 --> 00:08:34.080
I think Valerie Taylor
maybe here,

00:08:34.081 --> 00:08:37.340
she has an NSF funded
organization called

00:08:37.341 --> 00:08:38.510
The Future Leadership and

00:08:38.511 --> 00:08:40.425
the Professoriate
intended, again,

00:08:40.426 --> 00:08:42.110
to take under-represented
groups and

00:08:42.111 --> 00:08:44.720
provide them with
experiences that

00:08:44.721 --> 00:08:46.790
allow them to compete
more effectively for

00:08:46.791 --> 00:08:49.280
tenured positions in academia.

00:08:49.281 --> 00:08:51.820
We'll be including
the Flip- hello Valerie.

00:08:51.821 --> 00:08:53.580
Thank you. Good. I'm
glad that you're here.

00:08:53.581 --> 00:08:55.460
We'll be including them as

00:08:55.461 --> 00:08:58.300
well in collaboration with
the University of Washington,

00:08:58.301 --> 00:09:00.640
which was one of
the Flip Alliance Schools in

00:09:00.641 --> 00:09:04.510
our first PhD Summit
later on this fall.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:08.500
So, let's get started.

00:09:08.501 --> 00:09:11.105
I do want to take
a moment to introduce

00:09:11.106 --> 00:09:14.365
my boss and the head
of MSR Labs and MSRAI,

00:09:14.366 --> 00:09:16.410
Eric Horvitz, to say a few words.

00:09:16.411 --> 00:09:18.685
So, welcome, Eric.
Good to see you.

00:09:18.686 --> 00:09:21.050
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Sandy.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:29.015
&gt;&gt; I wanted to add
a personal welcome.

00:09:29.016 --> 00:09:32.820
Thanks for joining us
it's so great to see

00:09:32.821 --> 00:09:37.810
so many familiar faces as
well as so many new faces,

00:09:37.811 --> 00:09:41.610
I'd love to meet over
the next couple of days.

00:09:41.611 --> 00:09:44.830
I'm so impressed by
the new approach

00:09:44.831 --> 00:09:48.300
to faculty summits being
focused dramatically.

00:09:48.301 --> 00:09:53.810
We used to have a much
larger events that span,

00:09:53.811 --> 00:09:55.220
that was kind of
representative of

00:09:55.221 --> 00:09:56.950
the wonders of Microsoft
Research which

00:09:56.951 --> 00:10:00.755
span all of computer
science closer labs.

00:10:00.756 --> 00:10:03.440
But I think the focused
events dramatically

00:10:03.441 --> 00:10:05.910
are much more
productive for folks

00:10:05.911 --> 00:10:12.110
and a little bit more
community-centric.

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:15.400
The AI event last year
was just fabulous

00:10:15.401 --> 00:10:16.410
and I'm expecting the same

00:10:16.411 --> 00:10:18.740
from the system event this year.

00:10:18.741 --> 00:10:22.810
Leadership and innovation
in systems and networking

00:10:22.811 --> 00:10:24.820
is just a critical area

00:10:24.821 --> 00:10:26.990
for Microsoft Research
across our labs.

00:10:26.991 --> 00:10:28.440
Of course, it's a critical area

00:10:28.441 --> 00:10:30.680
for Microsoft corporation.

00:10:30.681 --> 00:10:33.555
So, it's great to see so
many of you here today,

00:10:33.556 --> 00:10:35.885
see so many people folks from

00:10:35.886 --> 00:10:37.680
our divisions as well as

00:10:37.681 --> 00:10:39.880
Microsoft Research as well
people who traveled out here.

00:10:39.881 --> 00:10:42.455
So, I'm looking forward
to that exchange.

00:10:42.456 --> 00:10:44.955
I'll actually be around
during the event.

00:10:44.956 --> 00:10:46.185
I'll be coming to dinner tonight,

00:10:46.186 --> 00:10:48.630
and have one of
these rare moments

00:10:48.631 --> 00:10:50.085
this afternoon to present

00:10:50.086 --> 00:10:52.745
a technical talk of
some work we're doing,

00:10:52.746 --> 00:10:57.930
with my smaller research team
on AI for AI systems.

00:10:57.931 --> 00:10:59.565
So, thanks everybody and

00:10:59.566 --> 00:11:00.990
thanks for all your work
and Sandy as well.

00:11:00.991 --> 00:11:03.310
&gt;&gt; Good. Pretty good.
Thanks very much.

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:07.060
All right. So, let's get started.

