00:12:33.106 --> 00:12:35.600
In some sense all our dreams

00:12:35.601 --> 00:12:37.324
have come true and you probably,

00:12:37.325 --> 00:12:40.190
I've heard this for
the last 20 years.

00:12:40.191 --> 00:12:42.080
Everybody in every year,

00:12:42.081 --> 00:12:44.330
we keep on saying it's
more through computing,

00:12:44.331 --> 00:12:46.770
the age of computing is here,

00:12:46.771 --> 00:12:50.120
but I'm going to share
a small anecdote.

00:12:50.121 --> 00:12:54.405
When I got my PhD in
the '90s, in the mid '90s,

00:12:54.406 --> 00:12:56.980
I graduated in Germany

00:12:56.981 --> 00:12:59.500
and I did something that is
called pointers whistling,

00:12:59.501 --> 00:13:02.665
and I got a 10 to
15 percent advantage

00:13:02.666 --> 00:13:04.060
out of my technique.

00:13:04.061 --> 00:13:07.710
I was very, very proud and
then I went to the US and

00:13:07.711 --> 00:13:12.105
the people said,10 to
15 percent, that doesn't matter.

00:13:12.106 --> 00:13:15.160
If it's not an order
of magnitude or

00:13:15.161 --> 00:13:18.250
at least a factor of
three, it doesn't matter.

00:13:18.251 --> 00:13:20.565
So, now, 20 years later,

00:13:20.566 --> 00:13:24.410
I can tell you that
one percent actually matters.

00:13:24.411 --> 00:13:27.580
One percent at the scale that we

00:13:27.581 --> 00:13:31.905
operate is huge benefits,
in any regard.

00:13:31.906 --> 00:13:34.560
So, one percent now matters
and, of course, now,

00:13:34.561 --> 00:13:36.745
thinking back about my PhD times,

00:13:36.746 --> 00:13:38.730
well 10-15 percent, yeah,

00:13:38.731 --> 00:13:41.035
right, if I would do that
today, this is great.

00:13:41.036 --> 00:13:44.540
So, just how good the times offer

00:13:44.541 --> 00:13:48.240
us for people who optimized
and I'm an optimizer person.

00:13:48.241 --> 00:13:49.930
These things matter now

00:13:49.931 --> 00:13:51.070
and everything that
we're going to

00:13:51.071 --> 00:13:54.375
do is going to matter much
more and this is great.

00:13:54.376 --> 00:13:57.860
Now, of course, this
comes with expectations.

00:13:57.861 --> 00:14:00.070
Now, when I do an optimization,

00:14:00.071 --> 00:14:01.670
then it needs to have huge,

00:14:01.671 --> 00:14:05.410
huge impact and that's
really why we are here,

00:14:05.411 --> 00:14:07.600
because we still kind of have

00:14:07.601 --> 00:14:09.765
opportunities for
orders of magnitude.

00:14:09.766 --> 00:14:11.330
But if they are not relevant,

00:14:11.331 --> 00:14:14.215
if they're not at the point
where it really matters,

00:14:14.216 --> 00:14:16.405
then it is irrelevant.

00:14:16.406 --> 00:14:18.950
One percent at the right place

00:14:18.951 --> 00:14:22.825
matters a lot whereas an order
of magnitude somewhere

00:14:22.826 --> 00:14:25.970
at an irrelevant place
doesn't matter.

00:14:25.971 --> 00:14:28.220
One of the things
that we are trying to

00:14:28.221 --> 00:14:31.050
achieve here together is kind of

00:14:31.051 --> 00:14:33.510
finding the places where
we can really push

00:14:33.511 --> 00:14:37.080
forward and make
a huge difference.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:42.760
So, Microsoft understands that.

00:14:42.761 --> 00:14:45.250
One of the things
that I want to kind

00:14:45.251 --> 00:14:46.970
of say at the beginning,

00:14:46.971 --> 00:14:49.080
I want to kind of
clear up with some of

00:14:49.081 --> 00:14:50.940
