more product groups
that we can even serve

00:24:22.831 --> 00:24:25.655
and we choose what we do.

00:24:25.656 --> 00:24:28.305
But when we do choose to
work with product groups we

00:24:28.306 --> 00:24:32.705
take different perspectives,
we take risks,

00:24:32.706 --> 00:24:35.360
we are more agile and that is- we

00:24:35.361 --> 00:24:37.830
are about taking
risks and of course,

00:24:37.831 --> 00:24:39.550
we collaborate with you which

00:24:39.551 --> 00:24:42.260
is- and we collaborate
with our competitors.

00:24:42.261 --> 00:24:47.400
We have very open discussions
and work with Google and

00:24:47.401 --> 00:24:48.605
Facebook and all of

00:24:48.606 --> 00:24:53.890
these researchers and engineers
in those organizations.

00:24:54.230 --> 00:24:56.780
I also want to comment
a little bit on

00:24:56.781 --> 00:25:01.040
our relationship with you.

00:25:01.041 --> 00:25:04.430
There are two forums
where we interact.

00:25:04.431 --> 00:25:07.050
One is in the scientific
communities and I think we

00:25:07.051 --> 00:25:10.030
are partners, equal
right partners.

00:25:10.031 --> 00:25:11.970
We write papers together

00:25:11.971 --> 00:25:14.385
and I hope we will
continue to do that.

00:25:14.386 --> 00:25:17.110
We serve on program
committees together we

00:25:17.111 --> 00:25:20.080
do all the service work
together and I

00:25:20.081 --> 00:25:24.430
think academics and
Microsoft Research researchers

00:25:24.431 --> 00:25:26.680
are interchangeable in
the scientific communities.

00:25:26.681 --> 00:25:28.735
I think most of you
don't even care in

00:25:28.736 --> 00:25:30.675
the scientific
community whether you

00:25:30.676 --> 00:25:33.450
are now talking to
Microsoft Research person.

00:25:33.451 --> 00:25:37.600
The other more
interesting relationship

00:25:37.601 --> 00:25:39.690
is the is the talent side.

00:25:39.691 --> 00:25:42.460
Of course, we heavily
depend on you

00:25:42.461 --> 00:25:45.075
because you are the source of all

00:25:45.076 --> 00:25:47.720
ultimately of all
talent but we also

00:25:47.721 --> 00:25:50.820
help you with our internship
program and we want to

00:25:50.821 --> 00:25:53.370
really intensify that and

00:25:53.371 --> 00:25:56.500
this is a huge part of
what we do and now it's

00:25:56.501 --> 00:25:59.645
summer and your interns are in

00:25:59.646 --> 00:26:03.475
Building 99 here and we're
having a great, great time.

00:26:03.476 --> 00:26:09.260
But I think we're also helping
you with this program.

00:26:09.570 --> 00:26:12.400
The little bit more
subtle things we

00:26:12.401 --> 00:26:14.460
also compete with you are,

00:26:14.461 --> 00:26:18.450
you are looking for recruiting
you junior faculty,

00:26:18.451 --> 00:26:20.275
we are looking for recruiting,

00:26:20.276 --> 00:26:22.670
so that's a little bit
of a healthy,

00:26:22.671 --> 00:26:25.660
I think, aspect of
our relationship.

00:26:25.661 --> 00:26:28.830
But what you shouldn't
forget is that we

00:26:28.831 --> 00:26:32.475
also create and grow talent
that you will hire later.

00:26:32.476 --> 00:26:35.580
So there are
many career trajectories

00:26:35.581 --> 00:26:38.900
where students go to
Microsoft Research and get

00:26:38.901 --> 00:26:41.500
this final boost
to then go back to

00:26:41.501 --> 00:26:46.830
academia and do really
outstanding research in academia.

00:26:46.831 --> 00:26:51.905
So with that, I want to come to

00:26:51.906 --> 00:26:53.380
the systems part and

00:26:53.381 --> 00:26:57.660
also introduce
our first keynote speaker.

00:26:57.661 --> 00:27:02.300
So, you've all seen
some pictures like

00:27:02.301 --> 00:27:06.730
that where there's- today
there's computing everywhere,

00:27:06.731 --> 00:27:08.520
we have this public cloud,

00:27:08.521 --> 00:27:11.565
we have private clouds and we
have all of these devices,

00:27:11.566 --> 00:27:14.770
and our CEO Satya Nadella,

00:27:14.771 --> 00:27:15.940
he often talks about

00:27:15.941 --> 00:27:19.190
the Intelligent Edge and
the Intelligent Cloud.

00:27:19.191 --> 00:27:21.780
So, the Intelligent Cloud
is of course,

00:27:21.781 --> 00:27:22.790
the public cloud and

00:27:22.791 --> 00:27:24.745
maybe some parts of
the private cloud,

00:27:24.746 --> 00:27:26.790
and the Edge are all
of these gazillions of

00:27:26.791 --> 00:27:31.135
devices that go around
and form one big system.

00:27:31.136 --> 00:27:35.750
As we are a company
and now I want to

00:27:35.751 --> 00:27:39.245
introduce our first
keynote speaker

00:27:39.246 --> 00:27:42.750
who essentially is
the mastermind of Azure.

00:27:42.751 --> 00:27:45.230
Azure is kind of the way

00:27:45.231 --> 00:27:48.830
we draw boxes around
these things.

00:27:48.831 --> 00:27:50.150
So Azure is of course,

00:27:50.151 --> 00:27:52.835
our public cloud but Azure or

00:27:52.836 --> 00:27:55.630
Azure Stack are also

00:27:55.631 --> 00:27:58.430
our private cloud
and the two together

00:27:58.431 --> 00:28:02.540
form the hybrid cloud
and Azure results

00:28:02.541 --> 00:28:07.270
are our IOT or Edge story
with the smaller devices.

00:28:07.271 --> 00:28:10.895
So, the mastermind behind that is

00:28:10.896 --> 00:28:16.190
Mark Russinovich who
is the CTO of Azure.

00:28:16.191 --> 00:28:20.360
He got his PhD from
Carnegie Mellon University,

00:28:20.361 --> 00:28:22.220
and since then, has had

00:28:22.221 --> 00:28:26.305
a very fruitful career with
many, many activities.

00:28:26.306 --> 00:28:30.680
So he's really passionate
about distributed systems,

00:28:30.681 --> 00:28:35.395
about security, about
operating systems.

00:28:35.396 --> 00:28:41.000
He's been working on Windows
and Azure obviously,

00:28:41.001 --> 00:28:45.035
for a long time and he
has created many tools,

00:28:45.036 --> 00:28:47.300
so he's the author of

00:28:47.301 --> 00:28:51.125
the system integration
administration tools for Windows.

00:28:51.126 --> 00:28:54.535
He had several stints,

00:28:54.536 --> 00:28:56.430
among others at IBM.

00:28:56.431 --> 00:29:02.580
But in 1996, he started
a company called Winternals,

00:29:02.581 --> 00:29:07.510
which was eventually acquired
by Microsoft in 2006,

00:29:07.511 --> 00:29:12.040
and since 2006 he has been
with Microsoft and has

00:29:12.041 --> 00:29:15.810
created essentially-
helped create

00:29:15.811 --> 00:29:18.920
Azure and holding Azure together.

00:29:18.921 --> 00:29:23.060
One of the very interesting
facts about Mark is that he's

00:29:23.061 --> 00:29:25.000
a very passionate
speaker but also

00:29:25.001 --> 00:29:28.220
a very passionate author
and he wrote books,

00:29:28.221 --> 00:29:30.480
not only technical books but also

00:29:30.481 --> 00:29:32.975
fictional books and
fictional kind of

00:29:32.976 --> 00:29:37.320
technical fictional books and I

00:29:37.321 --> 00:29:41.860
haven't read them but I've-
now I have to- three books.

00:29:41.861 --> 00:29:44.560
But I've actually looked in
it and I'm going to read

00:29:44.561 --> 00:29:47.430
one of them and you can
you can look them up.

00:29:47.431 --> 00:29:48.960
So with that, please welcome

00:29:48.961 --> 00:29:51.660
Mark Russinovich.
Thank you very much.

00:29:51.661 --> 00:29:53.980
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much.

